<h2 id="bg-col-01">BG-COL-01: Keep the theme colour and identity roles separate</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to theme selection, household personalisation and separate project identity assets.

Use only the seven [GOV.UK theme colours](colour.html). Choosing a theme colour must not add a project logo or replace a household name. Keep a family crest or business logo in its own colours and choose a suitable background rather than recolouring it. For example, switching to Green leaves the “Morgan household” title as it is.

civica uses the GOV.UK colour palette. That does not make civica an official government service, and GOV.UK’s identity rules do not apply to civica. The [project identity rules](brand.html) cover civica documentation and promotion. Follow [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html) for a family name, crest or business logo inside a home.


<h2 id="bg-col-02">BG-COL-02: Choose a colour by its job</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to interface colour tokens and semantic states.

Give identity, reading, interaction, state and data their own roles. The theme colour supports navigation and interaction hierarchy. Text tokens support reading. Focus and validation tokens mark an interaction state. Device colours may express a native domain state. Chart colours identify a source.

The Red theme colour is the same as the error colour (`#CA3535` in light mode, `#F0C6C6` in dark mode), and the Green theme colour is the same as the success colour (`#0F7A52`). With either selected, do not let a button or highlight stand in for an error or success: say it in words. A green primary is not proof of success, and a yellow lighting scene is a light recipe, not a warning. Choose a semantic fill and its paired text colour together. A dark-mode error text colour is not automatically a safe button background.


<h2 id="bg-col-03">BG-COL-03: Test combinations and retain another cue</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to rendered text, controls and meaningful graphical objects.

Test the real foreground and background pair against the [accessibility requirements](../accessibility/index.html). Normal text needs 4.5:1 contrast and large text needs 3:1. Meaningful control boundaries and graphical objects need 3:1 where the WCAG criterion applies. A quiet divider does not replace a visible control boundary.

Give a status words, a selection a checked or current state, and a chart series a name plus a pattern or shape. For example, show “Front door locked” in text as well as a green icon. Test focus on its real surface, including a dark masthead. A colour-blindness simulation helps a review but does not prove that everyone can tell every pair apart.


<h2 id="bg-col-04">BG-COL-04: Separate dividers from editable boundaries</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to structural borders, inputs and native control geometry.

Use `civica-border-quiet` for structural dividers and quiet card edges: `#CECECE` in light mode, with a documented dark counterpart. Use `civica-border-interactive` for boundaries people need to recognise a control, and `civica-input-border` for editable fields. Keep the strong input and focus treatments.

Do not outline every dashboard region in black. Group content with headings and spacing first. Equally, do not lighten every border: a text field, a focus ring and a card separator do different jobs. Keep native circle and pill shapes where they carry meaning, as on a switch, radio, badge, avatar or gauge.


<h2 id="bg-col-05">BG-COL-05: Preserve device, warning and notification meaning</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to device state, manufacturer identifiers and attention counts.

Keep the native icon and state colours for a device, and keep the device name and state readable. An unavailable sensor is not a zero reading and not an off device. A manufacturer mark can help identify a device. It is content, not the identity of the interface.

The [notification badge](../components/notification-badge.html) keeps its fixed attention colour and rounded shape with every theme colour. Do not treat its count as an alarm severity, and do not replace a warning message with a coloured dot. Confirmed success, a pending action and a failure each need their own wording as well as their own look.


<h2 id="bg-col-06">BG-COL-06: Review every display context</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to affected components with every theme colour, in both modes and in accessibility contexts.

The theme colour, and Light or Dark, are independent choices. Use the documented derived dark tokens and keep the exact theme colour on the brand header. Do not make a colour lighter by adding transparency.

Let forced-colour modes use system colours for text, links, focus and controls. Keep borders, labels and symbols if fills disappear, and avoid a blanket `forced-color-adjust: none`. Check real native dialogs, dense settings tables and editors, not only the spacious examples. A documentation preview is not an audit of all fourteen combinations (7 theme colours, 2 modes).


<h2 id="bg-col-07">BG-COL-07: Keep chart and map scales independent</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to categorical chart identities and proposed quantitative map/chart scales.

Operational charts take their colours from the GOV.UK chart palette, assigned by stable identity, in [Chart colours](chart-colour.html). Choosing a theme colour must not change what a grid, solar or device series looks like. Mode may change its tested rendering. Categorical colours identify different things, a sequential scale shows increasing values and a divergent scale needs a meaningful centre. Do not use a rainbow of categorical colours to suggest a measured progression.

For a new heatmap or map layer, write down its scale, units, endpoints and unavailable state before choosing colours. These are reference requirements. They do not mean every native map layer has been rethemed. Keep the source credit and a text route to the data.


<h2 id="bg-col-08">BG-COL-08: Adapt the output channel without importing identity</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to screen, promotional and print colour output.

Dashboard controls need calm colour for repeated use. A release graphic may use a larger headline or a limited illustration palette, but it must still read as civica and keep accessible text. Do not import GOV.UK social colours, accent-dot animations or official headers as a second theme.

For print, treat the supplied CMYK and RAL or descriptive values as references, not measured matches. Use screen hex in sRGB for the application and check a separate proof for printed material. Never change a screen token to approximate a paint or print sample. Illustration colour follows [Illustration](illustration.html).


## Review and evidence

The source inventory and rule checklist are in `docs/data/brand-guidance-inventory.json` and `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`. A reviewed source supports a design decision. It is not a pass for an interface. Before closing a checklist item, record the page or component, the theme and mode, the test result and any remaining issue. Check again after a change to a source, palette, component or renderer.
